1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,759
(Q31) Good morning, everyone.

2
00:00:01,759 --> 00:00:09,574
In the last few lectures I've been dealing with business finance, but now I'm going to move on to business systems.

3
00:00:09,574 --> 00:00:18,930
And in today's lecture I'm going to talk about what can go wrong when businesses try to copy their own best practices.

4
00:00:18,930 --> 00:00:27,555
Once a business has successfully introduced a new process - managing a branch bank, say, or selling a new product -

5
00:00:27,555 --> 00:00:35,870
the parent organisation naturally wants to repeat that success, and capture it if possible on a bigger scale.

6
00:00:35,870 --> 00:00:43,571
The goal, then, is to utilise existing knowledge and not to generate new knowledge.

7
00:00:43,571 --> 00:00:52,639
[Q31答案句] It's a less glamorous activity than pure innovation, but it actually happens more often, as a matter of fact.

8
00:00:52,639 --> 00:01:01,497
(Q32) However, surprisingly, getting things right the Second time is not necessarily any simpler than it was the first time.

9
00:01:01,497 --> 00:01:12,394
Now, there's been a lot of research into how companies can repeat their previous successes, and it certainly hasn't been confined to the United States.

10
00:01:12,394 --> 00:01:28,724
It seems that most large industries are trying to repeat their own successes, and manage the knowledge they've acquired - but even so it has been shown that the overwhelming majority of attempts fail.

11
00:01:28,724 --> 00:01:43,144
[32答案句] A host of studies confirm this, covering a wide range of business settings: branch banks, retail stores, real estate agencies, factories, call centres, to name but a few.

12
00:01:43,144 --> 00:01:49,972
(Q33) So why do so few managers get things right the second or third time?

13
00:01:49,972 --> 00:02:00,789
Let's consider one reason for failure - placing too much trust in the people who are running the successful operation, the 'experts' shall we say.

14
00:02:00,789 --> 00:02:14,797
Managers who want to apply existing knowledge typically start off by going to an expert - such as the person who designed and is running a successful department store - and picking their brains.

15
00:02:14,797 --> 00:02:25,519
[Q33答案句] Now, this approach can be used if you want to gain a rough understanding of a particular system, or understand smaller, isolated problems.

16
00:02:25,519 --> 00:02:38,970
The trouble is, even the expert doesn't fully grasp the whole thing because when it comes to complex systems, the individual components of the process are interwoven with one another.

17
00:02:38,970 --> 00:02:44,231
(Q34) The expert never has complete access to the necessary information.

18
00:02:44,231 --> 00:02:53,691
And the situation's complicated even further by the fact that experts are usually not aware of their own ignorance.

19
00:02:53,691 --> 00:03:03,334
The ignorance can take various forms. For instance, a lot of details of the system are invisible to managers.

20
00:03:03,334 --> 00:03:14,037
[Q33答案句] Some may be difficult to describe - learned on the job and well known by workers perhaps, but impossible to describe in a way that's helpful.

21
00:03:14,037 --> 00:03:25,277
And there are some things that people know or do that they're not even aware of.

22
00:03:25,277 --> 00:03:37,115
(Q35(notes填空)) Now, let's consider two types of mistake that can occur when a manager actually starts to set up a duplicate system to replicate a successful process.

23
00:03:37,115 --> 00:03:46,949
Firstly, perhaps he forgets that he was just trying to copy another process, and starts trying to improve on it.

24
00:03:46,949 --> 00:03:57,340
[Q35答案句] Another mistake is trying to use the best parts of various different systems, in the hope of creating the perfect combination.

25
00:03:57,340 --> 00:04:06,185
(Q36) Unfortunately, attempts like these usually turn out to be misguided and lead to problems.

26
00:04:06,185 --> 00:04:16,937
Why? Well, for various reasons. Perhaps there weren't really any advantages after all, because the information wasn't accurate.

27
00:04:16,937 --> 00:04:22,754
Or perhaps the business settings weren't really comparable.

28
00:04:22,754 --> 00:04:31,548
More typically, the advantages are real enough, but there are also disadvantages that have been overlooked.

29
00:04:31,548 --> 00:04:37,999
[Q36答案句] For example, the modifications might compromise safety in some way.

30
00:04:37,999 --> 00:04:49,159
(Q37) So, what's the solution? Well, I don't intend to suggest that it's easy to get things right the second time, it's not.

31
00:04:49,159 --> 00:04:59,763
But the underlying problem has more to do with attitudes than the actual difficulty of the task, and there are ways of getting it right.

32
00:04:59,763 --> 00:05:09,834
[Q37答案句] These involve adjusting attitudes, first of all, being more realistic and cautious really.

33
00:05:09,834 --> 00:05:18,449
(Q38) [Q38答案句] Secondly, they involve exerting strict controls on the organisational and operational systems.

34
00:05:18,449 --> 00:05:24,664
(Q39) And this in turn means copying the original as closely as possible.

35
00:05:24,664 --> 00:05:35,971
(Q40) [Q39&40答案句] Not merely duplicating the physical characteristics of the factory, but also duplicating the skills that the original employees had.

36
00:05:35,971 --> 00:05:38,471
Reliance on a template like this offers the huge advantage of built-in consistency.

